WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:03.159
THE STATE OF ANTI BIG TECH:
Nowaday’s it’s cool to hate on all big

00:00:03.159 --> 00:00:04.210
tech companies.

00:00:04.210 --> 00:00:05.790
Apple, overpriced.

00:00:05.790 --> 00:00:07.370
Google, evil.

00:00:07.370 --> 00:00:09.110
Facebook, lizard man.

00:00:09.110 --> 00:00:10.590
Amazon, bald man.

00:00:10.590 --> 00:00:15.600
And Netflix, well, according to the media,
they’re already dead.

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:20.720
Much of the criticism that these companies
receive is perfectly valid.

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:25.400
They do indeed collect user data, work to
keep us addicted to mind numbing content,

00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:28.099
and some of them have a horrendous workplace.

00:00:28.099 --> 00:00:33.450
But, while people like to complain about these
attributes, it’s not like anyone is switching

00:00:33.450 --> 00:00:35.160
away from these companies right?

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:40.610
I mean, in terms of revenue, all of these
companies are at all times highs or near all

00:00:40.610 --> 00:00:42.180
time high levels.

00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:47.071
And in terms of market cap, even with the
stock market crash and tons of layoffs, all

00:00:47.071 --> 00:00:50.840
of these compnaies are still worth hundreds
of billions if not trillions.

00:00:50.840 --> 00:00:56.120
But, while these companies are still performing
extraordinarily well, there seems to be a

00:00:56.120 --> 00:01:02.980
new movement within the tech industry that’s
also thriving: the anti-big tech tech industry.

00:01:02.980 --> 00:01:06.950
Most of these companies don’t create anything
ground breaking or revolutionary.

00:01:06.950 --> 00:01:11.860
They’re usually just rudimentary photo sharing,
emailing, or messaging services.

00:01:11.860 --> 00:01:17.880
But, that’s ok because their value proposition
lies in what they don’t do.

00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:21.210
DuckDuckGo doesn’t collect your browsing
data.

00:01:21.210 --> 00:01:24.890
Signal doesn’t spy on your personal conversations.

00:01:24.890 --> 00:01:30.580
And BeReal doesn’t serve you a constant
stream thirst traps and instagram thots.

00:01:30.580 --> 00:01:35.270
Given the general distaste for big tech products,
a large number of people have embraced these

00:01:35.270 --> 00:01:36.890
new platforms.

00:01:36.890 --> 00:01:39.299
DuckDuckGo has 80 million users.

00:01:39.299 --> 00:01:41.440
Signal has 40 million users.

00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:44.560
And BeReal has 47 million users.

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:50.350
For a brief period of time, BeReal was even
the most downloaded app on the Apple App Store.

00:01:50.350 --> 00:01:55.600
At this rate, these services can surely be
a viable alternative to big tech right?

00:01:55.600 --> 00:02:00.920
Or at least, they should get big tech to change
some of their policies and tendencies, but

00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:03.439
this is where the bad news comes in.

00:02:03.439 --> 00:02:09.190
Just as fast as these apps rose in popularity,
they’re also crashing in popularity.

00:02:09.190 --> 00:02:15.830
BeReal for example was able to reach an impressive
53 million installs, but only a mere 9% of

00:02:15.830 --> 00:02:18.550
users actually use the app on a regular basis.

00:02:18.550 --> 00:02:21.450
A similar story can be seen with Signal as
well.

00:02:21.450 --> 00:02:25.690
While their total user count keeps souring,
their daily active users was actually down

00:02:25.690 --> 00:02:28.959
60% by the end of 2021.

00:02:28.959 --> 00:02:32.030
This is in stark contrast to big tech companies.

00:02:32.030 --> 00:02:36.940
Though people apparently cannot stand their
practices, their user bases are stronger than

00:02:36.940 --> 00:02:37.940
ever.

00:02:37.940 --> 00:02:42.260
Google and Facebook literally have monthly
active user counts in the billions and it’s

00:02:42.260 --> 00:02:44.050
only expected to go up.

00:02:44.050 --> 00:02:48.910
So, here’s why anti big tech companies are
already failing.

00:02:48.910 --> 00:02:53.910
ROCKY FOUNDATIONS:
People ditching these anti big tech apps may

00:02:53.910 --> 00:02:58.950
seem like a new trend, but their eventual
demise can usually be traced back to the root

00:02:58.950 --> 00:03:00.030
of their success.

00:03:00.030 --> 00:03:04.080
You see, most of these apps don’t grow in
a very natural manner.

00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:08.620
It’s not like they grow from 1 to 2 to 5
to 10 million users.

00:03:08.620 --> 00:03:13.750
Rather, its more like, they have 5000 users
one months and then 10 million the next month

00:03:13.750 --> 00:03:16.090
then back to 1 million a year after.

00:03:16.090 --> 00:03:21.720
In other words, much of their popularity is
due to some sort of shortlived external surge.

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:23.000
Take Signal, for instance.

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:28.189
Signal has actually been out for nearly 10
years having been launched in July of 2014.

00:03:28.189 --> 00:03:34.629
But, most of you probably didn’t hear about
Signal up until 2021 and that’s no coincidence.

00:03:34.629 --> 00:03:40.350
Much of Signal’s popularity can be attributed
to a single tweet from Elon Musk: Use Signal.

00:03:40.350 --> 00:03:44.970
The original tweet itself didn't go viral
only garnering a few hundred thousand likes,

00:03:44.970 --> 00:03:50.569
but it got the attention of the media, social
media influencers, and eventually everyday

00:03:50.569 --> 00:03:51.569
people.

00:03:51.569 --> 00:03:55.590
And if you’re not convinced that this tweet
had much of impact, just take a look at Signal’s

00:03:55.590 --> 00:03:56.590
monthly downloads.

00:03:56.590 --> 00:04:02.060
As you can see, throughout 2020, Signal was
chilling at a few million downloads per month.

00:04:02.060 --> 00:04:08.000
Suddenly in the first quarter of 2021 though,
when Elon made his tweet, it exploded to 80

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:09.000
million downloads.

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:13.829
But, within just one more quarter, Signal’s
downloads were back down to a slightly higher

00:04:13.829 --> 00:04:14.829
baseline.

00:04:14.829 --> 00:04:18.470
And by the end of the year, the majority of
the people who had joined in on this sudden

00:04:18.470 --> 00:04:20.489
surge had already left.

00:04:20.489 --> 00:04:24.050
A similar story can be see with BeReal as
well.

00:04:24.050 --> 00:04:28.740
Most of us associate BeReal with 2022, but
the app actually launched at the start of

00:04:28.740 --> 00:04:29.840
2020.

00:04:29.840 --> 00:04:34.800
It wasn’t until a brilliant marketing effort
in 2022, however, that it actually blew up.

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:39.020
You see, Signal figured that most of their
potential user base was young adults.

00:04:39.020 --> 00:04:41.870
And what’s the easiest way to get to these
people?

00:04:41.870 --> 00:04:44.190
Well, the answer is college campuses.

00:04:44.190 --> 00:04:48.830
In early 2022, BeReal started paying college
students to promote the app by writing about

00:04:48.830 --> 00:04:53.000
in the school newspaper, hosting BeReal parties,
and sponsoring food.

00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:57.550
As the app gained popularity amongst college
campuses, the mainstream media started picking

00:04:57.550 --> 00:05:00.690
up on it and it really just snowballed from
there.

00:05:00.690 --> 00:05:06.300
But, at the end of the day, the app’s entire
user base could be traced back to one successful

00:05:06.300 --> 00:05:11.590
marketing campaign, and as that marketing
campaign lost steam, so did the app.

00:05:11.590 --> 00:05:14.090
The same thing could be said about Clubhouse
as well.

00:05:14.090 --> 00:05:19.650
In early 2021 when big name players like Elon
Musk jumped on, the platform went absolutely

00:05:19.650 --> 00:05:24.490
wild, but within just one year, I mean just
look at the graph.

00:05:24.490 --> 00:05:29.370
The reality is that while people love to complain
about big tech all day, it’s not until an

00:05:29.370 --> 00:05:33.830
external factor pushes them that they actually
try something new whether that be an Elon

00:05:33.830 --> 00:05:36.000
Musk tweet or a marketing campaign.

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:41.150
But, while such events are great a creating
buzz around the service, it doesn't build

00:05:41.150 --> 00:05:42.860
a loyal user base.

00:05:42.860 --> 00:05:47.400
People aren’t using BeReal or Clubhouse
for the messaging functionality or the chatting

00:05:47.400 --> 00:05:49.710
functionality or the social media aspect.

00:05:49.710 --> 00:05:54.500
Really, they don’t use it for any specific
purpose other than some celebrity is using

00:05:54.500 --> 00:05:57.050
it or everyone around me is using it.

00:05:57.050 --> 00:06:02.620
In other words, for most users, these apps
lack any real substance and are usually built

00:06:02.620 --> 00:06:04.610
upon a pretty rocky foundation.

00:06:04.610 --> 00:06:09.680
So, as soon as that external factor starts
to fade, so does the app.

00:06:09.680 --> 00:06:15.490
BECOMING THE VILLAIN:
Most of these apps don’t survive the virality

00:06:15.490 --> 00:06:19.140
boom, but even if they do, that’s just the
first step.

00:06:19.140 --> 00:06:24.199
Now, these apps have to live up to the promise
of being better than big tech offerings.

00:06:24.199 --> 00:06:27.990
Afterall, for many of them, that’s their
entire pitch.

00:06:27.990 --> 00:06:31.470
BeReal is not another social media network.

00:06:31.470 --> 00:06:33.770
Robinhood is investing for all.

00:06:33.770 --> 00:06:39.270
And Signal is the private WhatsApp, though
ironically it was created by one of WhatsApp’s

00:06:39.270 --> 00:06:40.270
founders.

00:06:40.270 --> 00:06:45.139
The problem though is that it’s virtually
impossible to stick to such virtuosic visions.

00:06:45.139 --> 00:06:46.930
I mean think about this.

00:06:46.930 --> 00:06:52.330
At one point in time, Facebook and Google
themselves were the anti big tech players.

00:06:52.330 --> 00:06:57.610
When Google launched, Yahoo, Microsoft, and
AOL dominated the search and browser scene.

00:06:57.610 --> 00:07:01.599
And by the time Facebook launched, Friendster
and MySpace were ginormous.

00:07:01.599 --> 00:07:06.230
Also, let’s not forget, Google’s entire
slogan was “Don’t Be Evil”.

00:07:06.230 --> 00:07:10.520
But, both of these companies eventually turned
to the quote on quote dark side.

00:07:10.520 --> 00:07:11.520
Why?

00:07:11.520 --> 00:07:14.639
Well, I can assure you that it’s not because
they want to.

00:07:14.639 --> 00:07:20.710
Do you think Mark Zuckerberg wants a 22% favorability
rating or that Google wants to be known for

00:07:20.710 --> 00:07:22.360
invasive data collection?

00:07:22.360 --> 00:07:23.360
Of course not.

00:07:23.360 --> 00:07:27.699
The reason that these companies turn to such
means is because that is literally the only

00:07:27.699 --> 00:07:29.319
way to do it for free.

00:07:29.319 --> 00:07:32.810
The only viable alternative is to charge end
users.

00:07:32.810 --> 00:07:37.910
But, if you wanna compete against free offerings
from big names, you have to also offer your

00:07:37.910 --> 00:07:41.650
service for free and that’s were the problem
arises.

00:07:41.650 --> 00:07:46.360
As an anti big-tech company, not only do you
have to offer your services for free to remain

00:07:46.360 --> 00:07:51.320
competitive, but you have to do it while not
engaging in any of the lucrative revenue avenues

00:07:51.320 --> 00:07:53.729
that the big tech companies engage in.

00:07:53.729 --> 00:07:58.510
This may work out fine for the first few years
when you have momentum and a bunch of venture

00:07:58.510 --> 00:08:03.750
capital, but eventually, you reach a crossroads
at which point, you realize that you have

00:08:03.750 --> 00:08:05.600
a fundamental problem.

00:08:05.600 --> 00:08:10.620
All of those big tech practices that your
app has disowned have led it to a dead end.

00:08:10.620 --> 00:08:12.129
Take BeReal for instance.

00:08:12.129 --> 00:08:16.560
The idea of the app is that users share a
simple photo of themselves everyday and that

00:08:16.560 --> 00:08:20.250
they don’t spend hours endlessly scrolling
through other people’s photos.

00:08:20.250 --> 00:08:25.190
But, bro, how the hell are you gonna make
money when you’re actively not just encouraging

00:08:25.190 --> 00:08:29.979
but enabling your users to use the app for
as little time as possible and not collecting

00:08:29.979 --> 00:08:31.280
any data on them.

00:08:31.280 --> 00:08:35.839
Let’s just say most of these apps have a
harsh reality check when VC money dries up

00:08:35.839 --> 00:08:40.090
and they have to continue paying for hundreds
of servers and thousands of engineers.

00:08:40.090 --> 00:08:42.779
At this point, you only have 2 options.

00:08:42.779 --> 00:08:47.350
Turn to the darkside and save the company
or continue pursuing your idealistic fantasy

00:08:47.350 --> 00:08:48.970
and kill the company.

00:08:48.970 --> 00:08:54.720
For obvious reasons, almost all founders and
leaders choose option 1 and before you know

00:08:54.720 --> 00:08:59.730
it, you’ve become the villain that you sought
to eliminate.

00:08:59.730 --> 00:09:03.160
FUNDAMENTALLY FLAWED:
Virtually no company is able to survive the

00:09:03.160 --> 00:09:08.630
virality boom and the funding crunch, but
hypothetically, let’s say some anti big

00:09:08.630 --> 00:09:10.680
tech company manages to do this.

00:09:10.680 --> 00:09:15.660
The reality is that they’re still screwed
because if the virality nor the funding took

00:09:15.660 --> 00:09:20.790
down the company, the users will because the
service is fundamentally flawed as it opposes

00:09:20.790 --> 00:09:21.790
human psychology.

00:09:21.790 --> 00:09:25.910
Here’s the thing, while it’s easy to blame
big tech companies for everything that’s

00:09:25.910 --> 00:09:30.500
wrong with their platforms, the truth is that
much of what’s wrong with their platform

00:09:30.500 --> 00:09:31.500
is us.

00:09:31.500 --> 00:09:36.190
Do you hate that your Instagram and TikTok
feed are filled with thirst traps or that

00:09:36.190 --> 00:09:40.110
your YouTube feed is filled with clickbait
titles that the economy is ending as we know

00:09:40.110 --> 00:09:41.110
it?

00:09:41.110 --> 00:09:44.579
News flash, this is not because of big tech
companies.

00:09:44.579 --> 00:09:48.310
These companies aren’t committed to showing
you any one thing.

00:09:48.310 --> 00:09:52.220
The only thing they’re committed to is keeping
you on the platform.

00:09:52.220 --> 00:09:57.339
So, the only reason your feed is filled with
thots is because that’s what you like to

00:09:57.339 --> 00:09:58.339
see.

00:09:58.339 --> 00:10:02.410
If you truly liked seeing documentaries about
kale and video essays about steel fabrication,

00:10:02.410 --> 00:10:04.990
that’s what these platforms would show you.

00:10:04.990 --> 00:10:10.440
Trust me, these social media platforms have
it down to a science and the almighty algorithm

00:10:10.440 --> 00:10:12.100
is just audience psychology.

00:10:12.100 --> 00:10:15.360
But, it turns out that you don’t like that.

00:10:15.360 --> 00:10:18.089
You don’t want to see authentic photos of
your friends.

00:10:18.089 --> 00:10:21.810
You want to see the highlight real because
that’s what gives you something to long

00:10:21.810 --> 00:10:23.940
for, something to fantasize about.

00:10:23.940 --> 00:10:29.910
While this is terrible for your mental health,
it’s also the most addictive and most engaging.

00:10:29.910 --> 00:10:35.600
But by virtue, this also means that an app
that circulates authentic photos is non addictive

00:10:35.600 --> 00:10:37.470
and non engaging.

00:10:37.470 --> 00:10:41.680
And though you may know that this is better
for your mental health, most people don’t

00:10:41.680 --> 00:10:43.149
end up sticking with this option.

00:10:43.149 --> 00:10:47.279
It’s the same reason that most people gain
back weight that they lose from a diet and

00:10:47.279 --> 00:10:49.000
why gyms are packed in January.

00:10:49.000 --> 00:10:55.610
While such apps have a cool premise, the reality
is that they’re essentially a social media

00:10:55.610 --> 00:11:00.370
diet, and sooner or later, most people stop
dieting.

00:11:00.370 --> 00:11:05.829
THE FUTURE OF ANTI BIG TECH:
At the end of the day, the massive fall of

00:11:05.829 --> 00:11:09.070
these anti big tech companies isn’t surprising.

00:11:09.070 --> 00:11:12.820
These companies are trying to break the law
of physics by trying to do it all.

00:11:12.820 --> 00:11:18.200
They want to offer the best parts of Google
and Facebook without paying the price, and

00:11:18.200 --> 00:11:21.540
that’s simply impossible because nothing
comes for free.

00:11:21.540 --> 00:11:25.680
If users aren’t paying with money, they
have to pay with their attention by watching

00:11:25.680 --> 00:11:28.639
ads and by sacrificing their privacy.

00:11:28.639 --> 00:11:29.870
It’s that simple.

00:11:29.870 --> 00:11:31.340
That’s how a business works.

00:11:31.340 --> 00:11:34.220
They offer a service or product for a price.

00:11:34.220 --> 00:11:37.820
And if customers aren’t paying, the business
is simply not viable.

00:11:37.820 --> 00:11:42.470
But, even if you somehow figured out the busines
side of things, you’ll be blocked by yet

00:11:42.470 --> 00:11:46.640
another law of physics: attention cannot be
created or destroyed.

00:11:46.640 --> 00:11:50.889
There’s a limited amount of attention out
there, and people like spending it on things

00:11:50.889 --> 00:11:53.410
that they know they shouldn’t be spending
it on.

00:11:53.410 --> 00:11:58.160
I mean, just think about it, the biggest restaurant
chain in the world isn’t a salad chain,

00:11:58.160 --> 00:11:59.860
it’s a burger joint.

00:11:59.860 --> 00:12:01.430
The same thing applies for tech.

00:12:01.430 --> 00:12:05.850
The biggest tech companies will always be
the ones that feed into your negative tendencies

00:12:05.850 --> 00:12:08.660
as opposed to the ones that try to oppose
them.

00:12:08.660 --> 00:12:12.820
And that’s why these anti big tech companies
are already failing.

00:12:12.820 --> 00:12:15.380
Would you ever attempt a big tech detox?

